(PAPER-TO-WORLD / AUTHORED DEMONSTRATION NOTES) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(One neuron in three dimensions: demonstration notes) Tj T*
(Page 4: Follow the signal down the axon.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Background teaching material. Not a published research paper.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Stronger input shortens the climb to threshold, so pulses leave the trigger zone) Tj T*
(more often.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Our simulation produced 33 spikes at 2 nA and 63 at 3 nA in the first 1 s \(0?1000) Tj T*
(ms\); the axon and terminal tags count the same window.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Reference targets measured over 1000 ms at tau=20 ms: I=0 nA -> 0 spikes; I=1 nA ->) Tj T*
(0 spikes; I=2 nA -> 33 spikes; I=3 nA -> 63 spikes. Approximate continuous-model) Tj T*
(rate for I>1.5 nA: f=1000/\(tau*ln\(R*I/\(R*I-15\)\)+2\) Hz. Finite observation time and) Tj T*
(discrete Euler updates cause small differences from this formula. Passing numerical) Tj T*
(agreement is not validation against a published paper or biological data. The 3D) Tj T*
(presentation uses exactly the same discrete update rule as the Python and 2D) Tj T*
(references.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This single-compartment model has one membrane voltage and no cable, so myelin and) Tj T*
(branch length cannot change the count. Our simulation gives 33 spikes at 2 nA and) Tj T*
(63 at 3 nA in the first 1 s \(0?1000 ms\), matching the checked Python reference.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Axon terminals: Where pulses arrive. The tag shows the firing rate from the first 1) Tj T*
(s.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Interspike interval: Time between two spikes; shorter intervals mean a faster) Tj T*
(rhythm.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Myelin: Insulating sheaths illustrated around the axon. Their geometry does not) Tj T*
(change this single-compartment calculation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
